## Daily routine

- have short, warm (not hot) baths or showers
- use your emollient to wash, and apply more straight after
- keep your emollient with you through the day

## Clothes and washing

- wear soft, breathable fabrics such as cotton
- use non-biological washing powder, and avoid fabric softener if it irritates

## Stop the itch

- keep nails short
- try tapping or pressing the skin instead of scratching
- cotton gloves at night can help children

## Sleep

Itch is often worse at night. Keep the bedroom cool, and apply emollient before bed.

## Fire safety

Emollients can soak into clothes, bedding and bandages and make them **catch fire more easily**. This applies to paraffin-free emollients too. Keep away from naked flames, do not smoke, and wash fabrics often at high temperature.

## Your mental health

Skin conditions can affect confidence, relationships and mood. Anxiety and depression are more common in people with long-term skin conditions. Talk to your GP if you are struggling. You can refer yourself to **NHS Talking Therapies** in England. **Changing Faces** supports people with a visible difference.

## Support

The **National Eczema Society** has a helpline and information.
